  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 308 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nNorthwesterly flow will persist mid-week through the following \nweekend as low amplitude ridging resides off the southern California \ncoast and deep troughing lingers across the Hudson Bay. While the \nforecast is largely dry for the latter half of the week, a subtle \nwrinkle could lead to some light precipitation potential Thursday \nthough confidence in occurrence is low. Approaching high pressure \nmay foster some weak fgen/moisture convergence on the leading edge \nand be enough to lead to shower and thunderstorm development. \nThere's no NWP solution more bullish on this than the 12z NAM12 \nwhich not only shows fairly expansive coverage of rain/thunderstorms \nbut also suggests rainfall amounts will exceed 1\" for many \nlocations. This seems fairly far-fetched given < 1\" precipitable \nwater values and uncertainties on enough lift to even materialize to \nlead to this activity. Even so, though to a much lesser degree, the \nearly morning ECMWF shows some modest QPF splotches in the area and \nlike the GFS, remains fairly inconsistent on this potential with \nwavering between subsequent model runs. There appears to be some \nensemble support with EPS/GEFS both showing non-zero probabilities \nfor measurable rain in the area though the NBM is very slow to pick \nup on this. For now, will keep PoPs limited to 10-14% which \nmaintains a dry forecast but is a sizable increase from previous \nforecasts.\n\nFriday into the weekend sees the beginning of a pattern change. Deep \ntroughing will dive into the Pacific Northwest as ridging begins to \nwane over the eastern Pacific. Ridge breakdown locally begins Friday \nand continues into the weekend as southwesterly flow resumes. A \npronounced h5 low should take shape by early next week which will \nlikely stall the feature and delay its arrival onto the Plains. \nMultiple lead impulses ahead of the parent trough should support at \nleast low-end precipitation chances late Sunday into Monday. With \nthe arrival of the main upper-level low, precipitation chances \nshould steadily increase. The NBM broadbrushes \"Chance\" categorical \nPoPs (35-45%) for late Monday into Tuesday and feel this is adequate \nfor now. Some timing differences exist within the global models so \nlater forecasts will likely see these values cleaned up some as \nconfidence increases.",
